you work them\"}]]}]}],[\"$\",\"tbody\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":\"Local markdown\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":[\"Text in one file per ticket under \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\".scratch/\u003cfeature\u003e/issues/\u003cNN\u003e-\u003cslug\u003e.md\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\", numbered blockers-first\"]}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":\"Top to bottom, by hand\"}]]}],[\"$\",\"tr\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":\"A real tracker (GitHub, Linear)\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":\"Native blocking links, or sub-issues where the tracker has them\"}],[\"$\",\"td\",null,{\"children\":[\"Any ticket whose blockers are done is on the \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"frontier\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\" and can be grabbed\"]}]]}]]}]]}]}]]\n62:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The edges live in the ticket either way. The tracker only decides whether anything can act on them in parallel. \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"to-tickets\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" produces the artifact; running it (one session at a time, or a fleet) is your job, not the skill's.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n63:[\"$\",\"$L52\",null,{\"level\":2,\"children\":\"The wide-refactor exception\"}]\n64:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"One shape breaks the tracer-bullet rule. A \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"wide refactor\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\" is a single mechanical change (rename a column, retype a shared symbol) whose \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"blast radius\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\" covers the whole codebase. One edit breaks thousands of call sites, so no vertical slice can land green.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n65:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"to-tickets\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" sequences that as \",[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"expand–contract\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\" instead:\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n66:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Expand\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\": add the new form beside the old, so nothing breaks.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Migrate\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\": move call sites over in batches sized by blast radius (per package, per directory), one ticket per batch, each blocked by the expand. CI stays green because the old form still exists.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"Contract\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\": delete the old form once no caller remains, in a ticket blocked by every migrate batch.\"]}],\"\\n\"],\"className\":\"ah-bullets\"}]\n67:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Where even the batches can't stay green alone, they share an integration branch and all block a final integrate-and-verify ticket. CI only has to be green at that ticket.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n68:[\"$\",\"$L52\",null,{\"level\":2,\"children\":\"Common questions\"}]\n69:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"It produced twelve tickets for a three-line change.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nOver-decomposition is the most reported problem with this skill, and many users see it. The \",[\"$\",\"$L53\",null,{\"href\":\"/ai-coding-dictionary/model\",\"dictionaryTitle\":\"Model\",\"dictionaryDescription\":\"The parameters. Stateless — does next-token prediction and nothing else. Cannot do anything agentic on its own.\",\"children\":\"model\"}],\" defaults to atomic units and loses the grouping that would make them meaningful. The quiz step is where you fix this. Ask it to merge tickets, and it will. There is also a lower limit. If the whole change fits in one context window, you don't need this skill at all. Go straight to \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-implement\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"implement\"}],\".\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"The tickets came out one per layer: all the schema in one, all the API in another.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThis is the failure the vertical-slice rule is written against, and the skill still produces it sometimes. Catch it at the quiz step by asking one question per ticket: what can I demo when this is done? A ticket with no answer is a horizontal slice. Some people add a \\\"demo path\\\" line to each ticket for this reason, and report that it pushes the model toward vertical slices.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"On GitHub the tickets weren't created as sub-issues of the spec issue.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThis was reported across a dozen runs and several models, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/issues/554\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"most fully in issue #554\"}],\". Fixed: \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"to-tickets\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" now makes each ticket a sub-issue of its source issue, and the GitHub tracker template gives the command (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"gh issue create --parent \u003cn\u003e\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\", \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"gh\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" 2.94+). Re-run \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-setup-matt-pocock-skills\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"setup-matt-pocock-skills\"}],\" to refresh an older \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"docs/agents/issue-tracker.md\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\".\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"\\\"Blocked by\\\" was written into the issue body instead of a real blocking link.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThis is the same kind of problem, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/issues/513\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"reported in issue #513\"}],\", where the agent even stated that GitHub has no native blocking relationship at all. It does: \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"gh issue create --blocked-by 12,15\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\". Because the skill publishes blockers first, their numbers are always available at creation time. The body text is meant to be the fallback for trackers with no native edge, not the default. The ticket template now omits \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"## Blocked by\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" when the edges are native.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":[\"Where do the local tickets go? The v1.1 notes said a root-level \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"tickets.md\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\".\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThey did, and that was a bug. A single shared file also caused race conditions when parallel agents wrote to it. Local mode now writes one file per ticket under \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\".scratch/\u003cfeature-slug\u003e/issues/\u003cNN\u003e-\u003cslug\u003e.md\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\", in dependency order, matching the layout the local tracker template already described. The \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"NN\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" prefix is a real ticket ID, so \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"/implement 03\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" works instead of retyping a long title.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"It kept truncating when it tried to read my spec.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nA very large spec can outgrow what a tracker issue serves back cleanly. There is no local copy to fall back on, so the agent spends \",[\"$\",\"$L53\",null,{\"href\":\"/ai-coding-dictionary/tool-call\",\"dictionaryTitle\":\"Tool call\",\"dictionaryDescription\":\"The model's output naming a tool and its arguments — just structured text. The harness has to read it and execute.\",\"children\":\"tool calls\"}],\" fetching chunks again and never reaches the end. Don't \",[\"$\",\"$L53\",null,{\"href\":\"/ai-coding-dictionary/clearing\",\"dictionaryTitle\":\"Clearing\",\"dictionaryDescription\":\"Ending the current session and starting a fresh one. The next message begins with an empty session and an empty context window.\",\"children\":\"clear\"}],\" or \",[\"$\",\"$L53\",null,{\"href\":\"/ai-coding-dictionary/compaction\",\"dictionaryTitle\":\"Compaction\",\"dictionaryDescription\":\"A handoff done in-memory: the previous session's history is summarised and seeds a fresh session. Lossy — detail traded for headroom.\",\"children\":\"compact\"}],\" between \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"/to-spec\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"/to-tickets\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\". Run them in the same context window and the agent never has to fetch the spec back.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"The acceptance criteria graded nothing: some passed before any work was done.\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThe template asks for criteria and says nothing about whether they can fail, so this happens. Three shapes recur: a criterion already true at the base commit, a criterion that only work in another ticket can satisfy, and one that restates the request rather than deriving from the artifact. Vertical slicing prevents most of it, because a slice that delivers new behaviour fails at the base commit by construction. The check is still worth doing by hand. For each criterion, name the observation that would show it false, and confirm it fails at the commit the implementer starts from.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n70:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"strong\",null,{\"children\":\"The tickets are published. How do I actually run them?\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-strong\"}],\"\\nThe skill stops at the artifact, and there is no auto-dispatch mode. Dispatch is manual: look at the board, count the tickets with no open blockers, and open that many agent sessions. Give each ticket a fresh context, and clear between them. \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-implement\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"implement\"}],\" does not reliably close or check off the ticket when it finishes, on GitHub or in local markdown, so you update the ticket's state yourself.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n71:[\"$\",\"$L52\",null,{\"level\":2,\"children\":\"It's working if\"}]\n72:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Every ticket has an answer to \\\"what can I demo when this is done?\\\", and the answer is behaviour, not a layer.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"The list comes back to you numbered, with a \\\"Blocked by\\\" line on each, before anything is published.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"The ticket at the top has no blockers and can be started immediately.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Nothing in a ticket body is a file path or a line number, except a snippet a prototype produced.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Each ticket reads like something a fresh session could finish without you in the room.\"}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":\"Prefactoring, where it found any, is at the front of the order rather than mixed into feature tickets.\"}],\"\\n\"],\"className\":\"ah-bullets\"}]\n73:[\"$\",\"$L52\",null,{\"level\":2,\"children\":\"Where it fits\"}]\n74:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"to-tickets\",\"className\":\"ah-code-inline\"}],\" is a step in the main build chain:\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n75:[[\"$\",\"$L54\",null,{\"moduleIds\":[\"4782351197561362667\"]}],\"$L79\"]\n76:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Upstream is \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-to-spec\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"to-spec\"}],\", which hands it a settled spec to slice against. Keep both in one context window, with no clear between them. Downstream is \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-implement\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"implement\"}],\", which builds one ticket per fresh session, driving \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-tdd\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"tdd\"}],\" for the tests and closing with \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-code-review\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"code-review\"}],\". \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-implement-spec\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"implement-spec\"}],\" is the other way down. It reads the same blocking edges as a task graph and builds every ready ticket in parallel on one integration branch. When you're unsure which skill or flow fits, \",[\"$\",\"$L6\",null,{\"href\":\"/skills-ask-matt\",\"title\":\"$undefined\",\"className\":\"ah-prose-a\",\"children\":\"ask-matt\"}],\" routes you.\"],\"className\":\"ah-prose-p\"}]\n77:[\"$\",\"$L7a\",null,{\"headline\":\"You have the skill. Now build the workflow around it.\",\"